{"api":1,"id":"sea-heat","engine":"sweep","units":"degrees Celsius, read from NASA's colour key","range":[0,32],"hotLine":28,"leftLon":-180,"words":{"subject":"ocean","map":"map of sea surface temperature","dates":"season","what":"warm water","top":"warmest","missing":"not read: NASA draws no current here, so it is land, sea ice or Antarctica."},"sweep":{"src":"data/02_sea_heat.json","chapter":"sweeps","mapping":"sea-heat","instrument":"pad","scan":"south"},"legend":{"summary":"A level scan line moves down NASA's map of the ocean's surface, from the Arctic to Antarctica, once for each quarter of the year from March 2007 to March 2008.","method":"On this track the scan line lies on its side: it runs from west to east and moves from north to south. So here your left ear is west and your right ear is east. The sound is a warm pad, after NASA's reference instrument, with a soft melody on top: its notes climb as the water warms. The colour of every current is read back to degrees Celsius on NASA's own key, from 0 to 32. Each sweep takes about 16 seconds.\n","items":[{"sound":"Deep base note","meaning":"Sea under the scan line. It is there over any water, alone over cold seas, and almost silent where the line crosses only land or ice. It is louder when more of the line is sea."},{"sound":"Temperature notes","meaning":"A soft swelling note every few degrees of latitude: its pitch is the temperature of the water under the line, one step of the scale higher for every two degrees. Warmer water sounds higher, colder water lower. Where part of the line is much warmer than the rest, it adds a second note in its own ear."},{"sound":"Chord voices","meaning":"Warm water under the line. The warmer it is, the more voices join on top, each one higher: a fifth from 6 degrees, more voices from 12, 18 and 24 degrees, and the highest from 28 degrees, the warmest seas on Earth."},{"sound":"Left and right","meaning":"Where along the line the warm water is: each voice leans toward it, west in your left ear, east in your right ear."}],"caveat":"Temperatures are read from the colours of NASA's flow lines, so they are the model's sea surface temperature as NASA drew it, to about one degree, and only where a current is drawn: land, sea ice and Antarctica have no reading. NASA's key starts at 0 degrees, so colder water near the ice reads as 0 to 2 degrees. Each sweep is one quarter of NASA's year-long movie, the middle value of every reading in it, so short changes inside a quarter are smoothed away.\n"},"about":{"what":"How warm the ocean's surface is, as NASA's flow lines show it, from the Arctic to Antarctica, in four seasons.","who":"Greg Shirah and colleagues at NASA's Scientific Visualization Studio, with scientists Hong Zhang and Dimitris Menemenlis. The currents and temperatures come from the ECCO2 ocean model (NASA and JPL).","when":"25 March 2007 to 3 March 2008.","where":"The global ocean. Land and sea ice have no flow lines, so no reading.","why":"To hear the shape of the ocean's warmth: the hot tropics, the cold poles, the Gulf Stream's warm tongue.","how":"A level scan line moves down the map. Each stretch plays a bell note whose pitch is the water's temperature: warmer sounds higher. Cold sea is a deep base note, and the warmer the water, the more chord voices join it.","journey":[{"title":"Satellites and ocean floats","detail":"Measure the sea's temperature and motion."},{"title":"ECCO2 ocean model","detail":"NASA and JPL's reconstruction of the ocean."},{"title":"NASA Scientific Visualization Studio","detail":"SVS 3912, Global Sea Surface Currents and Temperature."},{"title":"NASA Earth Information Center","detail":"The NASA exhibit and website whose videos and images this challenge starts from."},{"title":"Hearth","detail":"Reads NASA's pictures and plays them as sound in your browser. Nothing is uploaded."}]},"places":{"2,3":"Anchorage","2,15":"Reykjavík","3,5":"Prince George","3,6":"Calgary","3,17":"London","3,19":"Berlin","3,20":"Warsaw